WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.152
Bringing it together.

2
00:00:01.432 --> 00:00:06.445
The case against agents by default rests on four facts you can now compute or cite.

3
00:00:06.725 --> 00:00:15.173
Success compounds across steps, so long tasks need very high per-step reliability, unless errors are detectable and recoverable.

4
00:00:15.453 --> 00:00:20.723
Loops cost more than their per-call price suggests, because context is resent and grows.

5
00:00:21.003 --> 00:00:25.419
Trajectories are hard to debug and need real operational investment.

6
00:00:25.699 --> 00:00:35.405
And published measurements, read carefully, show capability rising fast on software tasks while consistency and real-world gains remain open questions.

7
00:00:35.685 --> 00:00:37.093
None of that says never.

8
00:00:37.373 --> 00:00:49.085
It says: start with code and a single call, add workflow structure where measurement shows a gap, and give the model the loop only for tasks that are complex, valuable, viable, and verifiable.

9
00:00:49.365 --> 00:00:56.576
The lab puts this into practice with a status-update pipeline built entirely from workflows, with tests for every branch.
